∙		6.	Visual Explanation (Diagrams When Helpful)

When a concept is complex, abstract, or involves system flow, you SHOULD include diagrams to improve understanding.

Use diagrams selectively — only when they genuinely help explain the idea.

When to include diagrams

Include diagrams when explaining:

∙	system architecture

∙	data flow

∙	request flow

∙	distributed systems interaction

∙	component relationships

∙	communication between services

∙	process steps

∙	comparisons between approaches

Do NOT force diagrams if unnecessary.

Diagram Types (Priority Order)

1.	Mermaid diagrams (preferred)

Use Mermaid syntax when possible:

∙	flowchart

∙	sequence diagram

∙	architecture diagram

∙	graph relationships

Example:

flowchart LR

Client → API → Database



2.	Simple ASCII diagrams (if Mermaid not suitable)


Example:

Client → Load Balancer → API → Database

Diagram Rules

∙	keep diagrams simple and clear

∙	do not overcomplicate

∙	must support explanation

∙	must include short explanation below diagram

∙	use Vietnamese explanation text

∙	diagrams must improve clarity, not decoration

